\fs36\b GNSS-SDR real-time positioning on Arduino UNO Q\b0\par
\fs24 with integrated GNSS-SDR Web Monitor (Node.js)\par
\fs22\par
\b Overview\b0\par
This document describes an embedded application of GNSS-SDR running in real time on the Arduino UNO Q platform. The system demonstrates GPS positioning using a compact, low-power Linux device, combined with web-based control and embedded visualization.\par
\par
\b System summary\b0\par
The Arduino UNO Q runs a Debian-based Linux distribution, which enables native installation of GNU Radio and GNSS-SDR. The complete GNSS-SDR software stack is built directly on the target following the official GNSS-SDR build and installation instructions provided by the GNSS-SDR project.\par
In its current configuration, the receiver supports GPS L1 C/A only. Multi-constellation operation (for example, Galileo) is not yet enabled due to computational and memory constraints of the platform.\par
\par
The system integrates:\par
- Real-time GNSS-SDR processing\par
- A Node.js web server for remote control\par
- A Python interface for data extraction\par
- An STM32F0 microcontroller driving an LED matrix for live visualization\par
\par
\b Hardware setup\b0\par
The hardware configuration used in this project consists of:\par
- Arduino UNO Q (Linux host with an STM32F0 microcontroller)\par
- RTL-SDR front-end (Nooelec NESDR SmarTee SDR)\par
- Active GNSS antenna\par
- LED matrix display driven by the STM32F0 MCU\par
\par
Figure 1 - Hardware setup of the GNSS-SDR application on Arduino UNO Q (see image in repository).\par
\par
\b System architecture\b0\par
The application follows a modular architecture that separates GNSS processing, control, and visualization tasks.\par
Figure 2 - High-level system architecture (see image in repository).\par
Main components:\par
- GNSS-SDR running on Linux, processing live RF samples\par
- Node.js web server for starting/stopping the receiver and selecting configurations\par
- Python interface script extracting GNSS-SDR outputs\par
- STM32F0 microcontroller receiving navigation data and driving the LED matrix\par
\par
\b GNSS processing configuration\b0\par
Real-time GNSS processing is performed using the following configuration:\par
- Constellation: GPS\par
- Signal: GPS L1 C/A\par
- Number of tracking channels: 7\par
- Coherent integration time: 4 ms\par
- Front-end: RTL-SDR (live RF input)\par
\par
The receiver computes standard GNSS outputs in real time, including GPS observables, carrier-to-noise density ratio (C/N0), and a Position, Velocity, and Time (PVT) solution.\par
Galileo signal processing is currently not enabled due to platform resource limitations.\par
\par
\b Web-based control interface\b0\par
A Node.js-based web server runs on the Linux side of the Arduino UNO Q and provides a browser-accessible interface to control GNSS-SDR execution.\par
The web interface allows the user to:\par
- Start and stop the GNSS-SDR receiver\par
- Select between different GNSS-SDR configuration files\par
  - Offline / post-processing configuration (TBC)\par
  - Real-time configuration using RTL-SDR\par
\par
Selected GNSS-SDR outputs (observables, C/N0, and PVT) are exposed for monitoring and downstream processing.\par
Figure 3 - Web interface for GNSS-SDR control (TBC) (see image in repository).\par
\par
\b GNSS-SDR config path\b0\par
The Node.js server resolves GNSS-SDR config files from an environment variable first, falling back to the current user's home directory:\par
GNSS_SDR_HOME=/home/arduino/gnss-sdr\par
If GNSS_SDR_HOME is not set, it defaults to ~/&lt;user&gt;/gnss-sdr via the OS user home.\par
\par
\b Embedded LED matrix visualization\b0\par
A dedicated Python script extracts relevant GNSS-SDR outputs and forwards them to the STM32F0 microcontroller on the Arduino UNO Q.\par
The microcontroller drives an LED matrix display, providing real-time visualization of receiver status and navigation parameters.\par
Currently visualized information includes:\par
- Satellite signal strength indicators (C/N0)\par
- Receiver activity and status\par
- PVT-related information\par
Figure 4 - LED matrix visualization of C/N0 (see image in repository).\par
\par
Figure 5 - LED matrix visualization of PVT information\par
The LED matrix provides a compact, color-coded visualization of key PVT parameters:\par
- Red: A binary clock indicating the number of tracked satellites. The most significant bit is the leftmost LED.\par
- Purple: A conventional PVT status indicator. It blinks when no solution is available, remains steadily lit at low intensity for a 2D fix, and steadily lit at higher intensity for a 3D fix.\par
- Yellow: C/N0 bar indicators for up to seven satellites.\par
- Green: Position and velocity representation. Within this square, a dot moves in the corresponding direction of motion; its displacement increases or decreases proportionally with the platform's speed.\par
\par
A working video can be found at: https://youtu.be/a7ZrCYMQSxc\par
\par
\b Status and limitations\b0\par
- GPS L1 C/A only\par
- 7 tracking channels\par
- Real-time operation validated\par
- Galileo and multi-constellation support: not yet enabled\par
- Quantitative performance metrics (DOP, accuracy): TBC\par
\par
\b Future work\b0\par
Planned extensions include:\par
- Performance characterization (C/N0 statistics, DOP, positioning accuracy)\par
- CPU and memory optimization\par
- Exploration of multi-constellation support\par
- Enhanced web-based monitoring and visualization\par
- Extended LED matrix display modes\par
